The JavaScript/TypeScript binding is one source that runs on Deno and Bun; all
runtime coupling lives behind a thin FFI/socket seam that loads the matching
adapter at startup. The binding reaches the installed shared pancake archives over
the host's FFI and mirrors the à-la-carte archive set as two packages.
`@pancake/stack`
([`bindings/ts/stack/mod.ts`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/ts/stack/mod.ts))
is the core: a full OCapN Host over the byte-fed host archive
`libpancake_driver` (the `Session`/`Server` surface, values, capabilities,
locators) plus the built-in transports `@pancake/stack/transport/tcp` and
`@pancake/stack/transport/inproc`. The optional `@pancake/iroh` package adds the
encrypted-QUIC transport, and the core's `connect` loads it lazily on the first
`iroh` locator. Each module opens only its own archive, so importing the core
never loads a transport. CapTP routing, three-party redemption, gift origination,
and far vows stay in the library; the binding moves bytes and honors the host's
action queue.

## What you need

- The packages are consumed from a pancake checkout today; there is no
  registry release yet.
- The binding `dlopen`s `libpancake_ocapn` and its transport siblings, so
  build them once with `zig build` in the checkout (the iroh tabs additionally
  need `zig build iroh-abi`, which needs cargo) and point
  `PANCAKE_LIB_DIR` at the build output (or install the archives on the system
  library path).
- Deno and Bun share one TypeScript source. CapTP smoke is gated on both
  (`zig build deno-dogfood` / `bun-dogfood`). Iroh aggregates
  (`bindings-iroh-loop` / `handoff` / `reachability` / `redeem-posture`) gate
  Deno; Bun is not a separate iroh member today. Run with
  `deno run --allow-ffi --allow-read`, add `--allow-net` for any program that
  dials or listens over TCP, and add `--allow-env` when `PANCAKE_LIB_DIR` does
  the pointing.

## The same round-trip

The [Start building]($link.page('start')) round-trip (connect, fetch, call,
handle the reply) reads like this in TypeScript. `serve` keeps the connection
dispatching until close; `fetch` and `send` start the inbound loop on demand.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import { connect, tcpLocator } from "@pancake/stack";
>
> // tcp: the peer is a place, a cleartext host and port.
> const partner = tcpLocator("127.0.0.1", 9099);
>
> const conn = await connect(partner);
> conn.serve().catch(() => {});
> const greeter = await conn.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> try {
>   const reply = await conn.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
>   console.log(reply);
> } catch (reason) {
>   console.error("the greeter refused:", reason);
> }
> conn.close();
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import { connect } from "@pancake/stack";
> import { Reachability } from "@pancake/iroh";
>
> // iroh reachability: the peer is a key, not a place. Discovery resolves
> // the bare node id over the open internet; no address in hand.
> const partner = "ocapn://3b6a…da29.iroh";
>
> const conn = await connect(partner, Reachability.Discovery);
> conn.serve().catch(() => {});
> const greeter = await conn.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
> try {
>   const reply = await conn.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
>   console.log(reply);
> } catch (reason) {
>   console.error("the greeter refused:", reason);
> }
> conn.close();
> ```

The iroh tab swaps the built host-and-port locator for a bare node id and adds
the posture import; everything below `connect` is the tcp tab, line for line. The
node id itself still arrives out of band, exactly like a host and port; discovery
turns a name you were given into a route, and it does not browse for peers.

The `serve` promise resolves when the connection closes cleanly and rejects on
a transport or protocol error. When the loop ends, every `send` still awaiting
a reply breaks too. Failures surface on the calls you await; the bare
`catch` only keeps the loop's own rejection from going unhandled.

## Hosting the other half

The hosting side reads the same way. `listen` binds a port and returns a
`Server` you iterate for each accepted `Session`, `publish` makes an actor
fetchable by name, and `serve` dispatches the partner's calls to it.

> []($block.attrs('code-tabs'))
> **tcp**
> ```ts
> import { listen } from "@pancake/stack";
>
> // tcp: bind a place you chose, a host and port.
> const server = listen("127.0.0.1", 9099);
> for await (const conn of server) {
>   conn.publish("greeter", (_verb, args) => `hello, ${args[0]}`);
>   conn.serve().catch(() => {});
> }
> ```
> **iroh**
> ```ts
> import { listenIroh, Reachability } from "@pancake/iroh";
>
> // iroh reachability: bind a key, not a place. The endpoint publishes its
> // identity, and partners dial the id from anywhere.
> const server = listenIroh(Reachability.Discovery);
> console.log(server.uri); // ocapn://<node-id>.iroh?direct-addresses=… — hand this to partners
> for await (const conn of server) {
>   conn.publish("greeter", (_verb, args) => `hello, ${args[0]}`);
>   conn.serve().catch(() => {});
> }
> ```

Over iroh there is no host and port to bind: `listenIroh` takes the posture,
and the server tells you its own locator through `server.uri`.

## Async

The async surface returns native `Promise`s and supports `using`, so a
connection cleans itself up at the end of a block. The `Session` is the
surface you hold: `await conn.send(ref, verb, args)` awaits a result there,
with no polling and no callback registration, and the handle a `send` returns
is itself a valid target for the next `send`, so a dependent chain pipelines in
one round trip. Beneath it, the connection's serve loop feeds raw socket reads
to the host's stream delimiter and honors its action queue.

## Actors

A hosted actor is a plain `(verb, args) => AppValue` callback: `conn.spawn`
gives it a capability, `conn.publish` makes it fetchable by name, and
`Host.publishGiftable` publishes it host-wide so a three-party gift can name
it. Every hosted object shares one native behavior trampoline; a throw becomes
the caller's broken promise, carrying the actor's own words.

## Dogfood and example

```sh
zig build deno-dogfood    # the Deno consumer against the pancake archives
zig build bun-dogfood     # the same source, on Bun
```

The full surface lives in the
[TypeScript binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/ts/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/deno/lib/room_client.ts)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
